d": "mag-src-qsl", "title": "2𝑘_𝐹 instability and chiral spin density wave at the 1/9 magnetization plateau in the kagome antiferromagnets", "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2512.11670", "authors": "Tanja Đurić; Pinaki Sengupta", "year": 2025, "version": "arXiv:2512.11670v1", "kind": "source", "status": "selected-text", "readDepth": "Selected conclusions and model-method overview in contents", "sectionsRead": ["Selected conclusions and model-method overview in contents"], "claim": "Neural quantum-state optimization and flux insertion favor a chiral spin-density wave over previously proposed plateau states.", "limitations": "Model/ansatz/finite-size-dependent theory; not a material proof and not an independent rerun.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "Neural quantum-state optimization and flux insertion favor a chiral spin-density wave over previously proposed plateau states.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/magnetism/qsl.html", "sha256": "b3e95074947a7a045d3b69156ccf003f00103afb486b4822dfdcdf2c10c81d28", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:26:11.255984+00:00", "doi": "10.48550/arXiv.2512.11670", "domain": "magnetism", "recordKind": "preprint", "sourceKind": "preprint"}</data></node><node id="mag-src-magnetoelectric"><data key="title">Layer dipole magnetoelectric polarizability of antiferromagnetic bilayers</data><data key="kind">source</data><data key="domain">magnetism</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "mag-src-magnetoelectric", "title": "Layer dipole magnetoelectric polarizability of antiferromagnetic bilayers", "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2512.05875", "authors": "H. Radhakrishnan; C. Ortix; J. W. F. Venderbos", "year": 2025, "version": "arXiv:2512.05875v1", "kind": "source", "status": "selected-text", "readDepth": "Selected abstract/introduction and conclusion", "sectionsRead": ["Selected abstract/introduction and conclusion"], "claim": "A theoretical layer-dipole response is introduced for PT-symmetric antiferromagnetic bilayers.", "limitations": "Its displacement-field/layer-pseudospin convention differs from bulk SI alpha_ij; selected theory only.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "A theoretical layer-dipole response is introduced for PT-symmetric antiferromagnetic bilayers.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/magnetism/magnetoelectric.html", "sha256": "16e8d0a673d950f249711b24fad286cad6be52c0866ee4871e68f87f7690e365", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:26:12.037377+00:00", "doi": "10.48550/arXiv.2512.05875", "domain": "magnetism", "recordKind": "preprint", "sourceKind": "preprint"}</data></node><node id="mag-src-ruo2-2026"><data key="title">[2611.09098] Disentangling Spin and Orbital Contributions in the Search for Altermagnetic Terahertz Conversion in RuO2</data><data key="kind">source</data><data key="domain">magnetism</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "mag-src-ruo2-2026", "title": "[2611.09098] Disentangling Spin and Orbital Contributions in the Search for Altermagnetic Terahertz Conversion in RuO2", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09098v1", "authors": ["Liu, Jiahao", "Mo, Yuxiao", "Guan, Tongyang", "Fang, Wenhui", "Cui, Yongwei", "Qin, Wentao", "Yuan, Zhe", "Zhang, Lishu", "Tao, Zhensheng", "Wu, Yizheng"], "year": 2026, "version": "arXiv:2611.09098v1", "kind": "source", "status": "selected-text", "readDepth": "Version1 PDF:abstract;introduction channel ambiguity;experimental principle/wedge sample and injector paragraphs;Discussion/Conclusion interface-control paragraphs read.", "sectionsRead": ["Introduction:ASSE/IASSE versus ISHE/OHE;experimental-principle and sample paragraphs near Fig.1;Discussion/Conclusion includingCu-spacer trend"], "claim": "Polarity/thickness and Cu-spacer controls distinguish spin/orbital conversion interpretations in the studied RuO2 heterostructures.", "limitations": "No independent raw THz trace, calibration, supplement or transport-model fit reanalysis. The result concerns a specified specimen/channel and does not prove universal absence of magnetic order in all RuO2.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "The source describes RuO2(101)wedge films,3nmFe/Ni/Py injectors and polarity/thickness discrimination. Cu insertion changes the measured anisotropy, supporting interface-conditioned interpretations rather than a unique bulk-order inference.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/magnetism/ruo2-2026-body.pdf", "sha256": "0e4faaee8ff19d1bfec961d42e81107906d137954fbd9842be415c5a1b689355", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:47:08.932342+00:00", "doi": "10.48550/arXiv.2611.09098", "domain": "magnetism", "recordKind": "preprint", "sourceKind": "preprint"}</data></node><node id="mag-src-moire-2026"><data key="title">[2611.31861] Superconductivity with valley-imbalanced Fermi surfaces in thick rhombohedral graphene</data><data key="kind">source</data><data key="domain">magnetism</data><data key="status">abstract-only</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "mag-src-moire-2026", "title": "[2611.31861] Superconductivity with valley-imbalanced Fermi surfaces in thick rhombohedral graphene", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31861", "authors": ["Liu, Le", "Zhao, Jian", "Xu, Cheng", "Watanabe, Kenji", "Taniguchi, Takashi", "Zhang, Yang", "Gao, Weibo"], "year": 2026, "version": "arXiv:2611.31861v1", "kind": "source", "status": "abstract-only", "readDepth": "abstract-only; full abstract and24Nov2026 submission record", "sectionsRead": ["abstract-only; full abstract and24Nov2026 submission record"], "claim": "In-plane-field-enhanced superconductivity and unequal Fermi pockets are reported in17-layer rhombohedral graphene.", "limitations": "Abstract-only very recent preprint; magnon-assisted equal-spin pairing is a motivated scenario, not uniquely established.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "In-plane-field-enhanced superconductivity and unequal Fermi pockets are reported in17-layer rhombohedral graphene.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/magnetism/moire-2026.html", "sha256": "8fb082241c9bdba924217889d57e08240e9974f54f2841d7d3fbbf20d9d3a84b", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:26:11.360671+00:00", "doi": "10.48550/arXiv.2611.31861", "domain": "magnetism", "recordKind": "preprint", "sourceKind": "preprint"}</data></node><node id="mag-src-gum"><data key="title">JCGM100:2008 Guide to expression of uncertainty in measurement</data><data key="kind">source</data><data key="domain">magnetism</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "mag-src-gum", "title": "JCGM100:2008 Guide to expression of uncertainty in measurement", "url": "https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/2071204/JCGM_100_2008_E.pdf", "authors": "Joint Committee for Guides in Metrology", "year": 2008, "version": "JCGM100:2008 corrected edition", "kind": "source", "status": "selected-text", "readDepth": "Selected covariance/combined-uncertainty section5.2 and coverage-factor caveat read", "sectionsRead": ["5.2 correlated inputs; distinction between standard and expanded uncertainty"], "claim": "Correlations and sensitivities belong in combined uncertainty.", "limitations": "A propagation approximation is not an automatic probability statement for arbitrary deterministic bounds.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "The formula dictionary uses a local Jacobian and an explicitly stated covariance model.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/magnetism/gum.pdf", "sha256": "41bbf068fbc0d7986c98691b2d1af6680cb3044f6a1a89b3560933ed9ef9626c", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:35:53.027839+00:00", "domain": "magnetism", "recordKind": "official-data", "sourceKind": "official-data"}</data></node><node id="mag-src-tong-phase"><data key="title">Statistical Physics §5: phase transitions</data><data key="kind">source</data><data key="domain">magnetism</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "mag-src-tong-phase", "title": "Statistical Physics §5: phase transitions", "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/statphys/statmechhtml/S5.html", "authors": "David Tong", "year": null, "version": "Online notes captured2026-11-25", "kind": "source", "status": "selected-text", "readDepth": "Selected named sections/slides read; not the full course or every embedded equation image.", "sectionsRead": ["5.2.1 mean-field derivation;5.2.3 validity;5.4 Landau free energy"], "claim": "Mean-field theory neglects connected neighboring fluctuations and can give false low-dimensional ordering or incorrect exponents.", "limitations": "Ising spin variables/field couplings are normalized model quantities, not automatically SI magnetization and tesla. The source does not establish every frustrated-material phase.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "The notes explicitly show where mean field is approximate, distinguish finite partition sums from thermodynamic nonanalyticity, and derive a self-consistent tanh response.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/magnetism/statmech-tong.html", "sha256": "e6735727eee6f0eaec54e25cef2c10ad078edbc5a30808d20bde83f07f40db9f", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:44:09.619934+00:00", "domain": "magnetism", "recordKind": "textbook", "sourceKind": "textbook"}</data></node><node id="mag-src-mit-london"><data key="title">Applied Superconductivity Lecture4: London equations</data><data key="kind">source</data><data key="domain">magnetism</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "mag-src-mit-london", "title": "Applied Superconductivity Lecture4: London equations", "url": "https://web.mit.edu/6.763/www/FT03/Lectures/Lecture4.pdf", "authors": "Terry P. Orlando / MIT", "year": 2003, "version": "6.763 Fall2003,15September2003", "kind": "source", "status": "selected-text", "readDepth": "Selected named sections/slides read; not the full course or every embedded equation image.", "sectionsRead": ["Slides on first London equation,penetration depth,perfect-conductor flux conservation;second London equation slide21"], "claim": "Perfect conductivity alone conserves original flux, while equilibrium superconductivity requires the additional flux-expelling constitutive premise.", "limitations": "Selected slide prose and equations as extractable text; several displayed equations are image-rendered and are not claimed as independent OCR transcriptions. Not a microscopic BCS derivation.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "The lecture distinguishes flux-conserving perfect-conductor response from superconducting flux expulsion and introduces the London penetration scale.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/magnetism/supercon-mit-lecture4.pdf", "sha256": "f50e28ee3e8e44c08386148b8a100bae2beccd25cd303615a9f03f751f85ffe4", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:44:50.941709+00:00", "domain": "magnetism", "recordKind": "textbook", "sourceKind": "textbook"}</data></node><node id="mag-src-mit-fluxoid"><data key="title">Applied Superconductivity Lecture10: supercurrent and fluxoid quantization</data><data key="kind">source</data><data key="domain">magnetism</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "mag-src-mit-fluxoid", "title": "Applied Superconductivity Lecture10: supercurrent and fluxoid quantization", "url": "https://web.mit.edu/6.763/www/FT03/Lectures/Lecture10.pdf", "authors": "Terry P. Orlando / MIT", "year": 2003, "version": "6.763 Fall2003,7October2003", "kind": "source", "status": "selected-text", "readDepth": "Selected named sections/slides read; not the full course or every embedded equation image.", "sectionsRead": ["Macroscopic wavefunction/supercurrent outline;London slide5;fluxoid slide8;simply/multiply connected regions;vortex overview"], "claim": "Single-valued phase constrains a fluxoid with a current term; magnetic flux alone is a further bulk-limit statement.", "limitations": "Selected slides only; no complete original Ginzburg–Landau upper-critical-field derivation or microscopic proof was reviewed.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "The notes retain current and flux contributions and distinguish contour topology, coherent normal regions and vortex-related quantization.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/magnetism/supercon-mit-lecture10.pdf", "sha256": "d61ebd2a8cd7d5c2c062c6eeb1127c8c8f1562aff93729ed49f6e393e4709b48", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:45:25.250093+00:00", "domain": "magnetism", "recordKind": "textbook", "sourceKind": "textbook"}</data></node><node id="mag-paper-ruo2-null"><data key="title">A meta-GGA perpective on the altermagnetism of RuO2</data><data key="kind">paper</data><data key="domain">magnetism</data><data key="status">selected-text-review</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "mag-paper-ruo2-null", "sourceId": "mag-src-ruo2-null", "topicIds": ["mag-ruo2"], "question": "A higher-rung r2SCAN-L calculation retains a nonmagnetic ideal bulk state while predicting strain/doping thresholds.", "method": "Selected sourceMethods:elk FP-LAPW;9×9×13k sampling;Liechtenstein+U/FLL;fixedJ=0.5eV;fixed-spin-moment comparison acrossfunctionals.", "result": "A higher-rung r2SCAN-L calculation retains a nonmagnetic ideal bulk state while predicting strain/doping thresholds.", "whatIsNew": "Source authors’ contribution: A higher-rung r2SCAN-L calculation retains a nonmagnetic ideal bulk state while predicting strain/doping thresholds.", "limitations": "FP-LAPW/functional and Hubbard-U-dependent results;finite Brillouin-zone sampling andfixed-spin-moment/local-sphere definition. No independentDFT rerun oruniversal experimental absence theorem.", "status": "selected-text-review", "equationsUsed": [], "replication": "Not independently replicated or computationally reproduced in this atlas pass.", "nextQuestions": ["Which independent measurement/model comparison could distinguish the principal competing explanation?", "Are the specimen geometry, disorder, uncertainty and code/data sufficient for reproduction?"], "title": "A meta-GGA perpective on the altermagnetism of RuO2", "kind": "paper", "domain": "magnetism"}</data></node><nod